# 23. Security Impact

The vulnerabilities found in the challenge can be grouped into several categories.

## Credential Exposure

Sensitive MCP credentials were stored in a local configuration file.

Impact:

```text
Unauthorized authentication
```

---

## Broken JWT Validation

The service accepted:

```text
alg=none
```

and trusted the JWT role claim.

Impact:

```text
User → Admin privilege escalation
```

---

## Arbitrary Code Execution

Administrators could register tools containing arbitrary Python code.

Impact:

```text
Remote code execution inside MCP container
```

---

## Excessive Kubernetes Permission

The ServiceAccount had:

```text
get nodes/proxy
```

Impact:

```text
Access to kubelet functionality
```

---

## Privileged Pod

A node-exporter pod was configured as:

```text
privileged=True
```

Impact:

```text
Significantly increased container privileges
```

---

## Host Filesystem Exposure

The pod mounted the host filesystem through:

```text
/host/root
```

Impact:

```text
Potential host filesystem compromise
```

---

# 24. Lessons Learned

This challenge demonstrated the importance of following evidence rather than assuming the first approach will work.

Several failed attempts provided useful information:

```text
403
 ↓
Investigate JWT

422
 ↓
Inspect OpenAPI

SyntaxError
 ↓
Understand code execution context

Permission denied
 ↓
Investigate container privileges

kubectl not found
 ↓
Use Kubernetes API directly

401
 ↓
Correct ServiceAccount authentication

pods allowed=false
 ↓
Investigate RBAC alternatives

nodes/proxy allowed
 ↓
Investigate kubelet

Privileged pod discovered
 ↓
Investigate host mounts

/host/root
 ↓
Host filesystem access
```

The key lesson was that every failed request reduced the uncertainty about the environment.

---

# 25. Final Takeaway

The root compromise was the result of chaining multiple security weaknesses rather than exploiting a single vulnerability.

The most important chain was:

```text
JWT validation weakness
        +
Admin functionality
        +
Arbitrary Python execution
        +
Kubernetes ServiceAccount
        +
nodes/proxy
        +
Kubelet
        +
Privileged pod
        +
Host filesystem mount
        =
Host-level access
```

The flag was successfully obtained, but its value has been intentionally **redacted from this public GitHub write-up**.
